READING — description
=====================

Specification of the Reading calculus, given by Marek.

Points marked [ASSUMPTION] were not fixed by the specification; they are the
reading adopted here so the definition is total. Correct them in place if wrong.
Points marked [OPEN] are unsettled and must not be resolved silently in code.

Contents: §1 what a reading is · §2 typing · §3 the initial step · §4 the two
steps · §5 summary · §6 invariants · §7 what a reading can build · §8 ONTOLOGY ·
§9 worked examples · §10 open points.

§8 (ontology) formalises what the `reading-interpretation` skill describes;
read that skill before working on §8.


1. What a reading is
--------------------

A READING IS A PROCESS — the process of creating it. It has two states.

    OPEN     while it is being created.
             An open reading is  R = (G(t), Pr):
               G(t)  a sharing graph in the sense of the GAL bus algorithm
                     (the `optimal_lambda/` package),
               Pr    a set of pointers to EDGES of that graph.

    CLOSED   once it is saved.
             A closed reading consists of the sharing graph and ITS ONTOLOGY SET
             (§8). It has NO POINTER SET — that is the only thing closing drops.

                 closed reading = ( G(t), ontologies )

The lambda term t is the record of the path taken; G(t) is how it is held. By
§2 a graph has a type, so a closed reading contributes a TYPE wherever it is
used. The ontologies are carried because they are SURVIVORS — hypotheses that
already passed refutation over the whole of that reading's construction (§8.8) —
and re-deriving them by enrichment could not recover that, since enrichment
proposes candidates by TYPE alone and knows nothing of what the reading confirmed.

A CLOSED READING IS DE FACTO EQUIVALENT TO A READING WITH JUST ONE POINTER, TO
THE ROOT OF ITS GRAPH G(t). The two states are therefore not two kinds of
object: closing COLLAPSES whatever pointer set was held down to the root, and
re-opening (§3(b)) merely makes that root pointer explicit again. Three
consequences, used throughout:

  - SAVING IS TOTAL. A reading may be closed from any state, whatever its
    pointer set.
  - RE-OPENING IS WELL DEFINED for every closed reading, and yields the single
    root pointer. It is not a special rule but an instance of the equivalence.
  - NO POINTER SET NEED BE STORED, because the pointer is recoverable from the
    graph alone.

  BUT THE ONTOLOGIES MUST BE STORED, and that is a real persistence requirement:
  `kg_store.save_readings_db` currently holds (name, term) pairs only, which
  suffices for the graph and the re-minted pointer but NOT for the ontology set.
  Extending that schema is a prerequisite for §8.3's initialisation and for §8.6's
  closed-reading operand, both of which read a closed reading's ontologies.

SCOPE OF THE EQUIVALENCE. It states what a closed reading IS, not that closed
and open readings are interchangeable everywhere. The steps of §4 act on an OPEN
reading, so a closed reading must be re-opened by §3(b) before it can be stepped
on; and where a closed reading appears as an OPERAND of contraction (§4.1) it
contributes its graph and type, never a position.

In an open reading, before each step the user selects one pointer from Pr. It is
called the ACTUAL POINTER, actPtr. IT REPRESENTS THE PLACE WHERE THE USER STAYS.

Pointers exist because of reflection: a reading acquires more than one pointer
only by reflecting (§4.2), which replaces actPtr by two. Contraction (§4.1)
re-points actPtr and neither creates nor destroys pointers. NO OPERATION REMOVES A
POINTER, so Pr grows monotonically and is never empty.

The shared graph is built BOTTOM-UP: every step replaces the subgraph at a
pointer by a larger one containing it.

AN ENTITY THE USER REUSES IS ONE NODE. G(t) is a SHARING graph, so an entity that
turns up again in a later contraction is not a second variable of the same name: it
is the same node, reached from every place it was used. Two kinds of sharing arise,
and they are not the same mechanism:

    ENTITY REUSE     the same entity used at several positions. Shared because it
                     IS one entity; nothing is cast and no position forks. There
                     is no fan — the node is simply reached more than once.

    REFLECTION       one SUBJECT viewed under two casts (§4.2). The sharing is the
                     step's whole content, so it is carried by an explicit fan-in
                     whose two aux ports are the reader's new positions.

  Sharing an entity does NOT tie the positions together: contracting at one
  occurrence leaves the others as they were. What is shared is the entity, not the
  context built around it — so the graph records one node with several parents,
  each parent free to grow independently.


2. Types are entities
---------------------

Typing here is NOT the typing of the usual typed lambda calculus. It answers one
question: WHERE IS THE USER STANDING?

  - Types are exactly entities of the knowledge graph. Written A, B, C, ...
  - Variables are written a, b, c, ...  Each variable has a type [a].
  - [t] denotes the type of the term t.

Typing rules:

    [a]           = A            for a variable a of type A
    [app(a, b)]   = [b]
    [(lam a) t]   = [a]

The second rule is the load-bearing one: the type is carried by the ARGUMENT.
Consequences used throughout:

    putting material in ARGUMENT position moves the type to that material;
    putting material in FUNCTION position leaves the type untouched.

An arrow A -> B denotes a relation (claim) of the knowledge graph with subject A
and object B. [ASSUMPTION] Selecting an arrow is what licenses a step: the user
may only act along relations that actually exist in the graph
(cf. `kg_store.get_claims_for_subject`, `kg_store.get_claims_for_object`).

Correspondence to code: `term_utils._term_type` implements exactly these three
rules and is normative. Verify typing claims against it.

The third rule gives an abstraction the type of its BOUND VARIABLE. Readings
themselves contain no abstractions (§7); the rule is stated because abstractions
arise in the ontology layer, which is specified separately.


3. Initial step
---------------

A reading begins in one of two ways. The user selects either:   

  (a) A TYPE A. The user stands at A:

          t = a,  where [a] == A
          R = ( G(a), {pointer to a} )

  (b) AN ALREADY SAVED READING — a closed reading:

          R = ( G(t), {pointer to G(t)} )

      The reading re-opens with a single pointer, to the whole of the closed
      reading's graph. The user stays at [t] (§2).

Both forms open a reading with exactly one pointer, so the generic steps of §4
apply from the outset.

Form (a) is the only point at which knowledge of the graph enters from outside:
the user names an entity with no prior reading to justify it. It presumably
corresponds to search or browse — a facility outside the reading calculus. Form
(b) needs no such justification, being the user's own prior work. Every later
step uses only what is already known: the arrows incident to the place where the
user stays, and the user's own closed readings.

Form (b) is what RE-OPENS a closed reading, and by the equivalence of §1 it is
not a special rule: a closed reading already IS a reading with one pointer at its
root, and form (b) only makes that pointer explicit.

Closing COLLAPSES the pointer set to the root (§1). A reading closed while
holding several pointers does not retain them, so those positions are not
recovered by re-opening — but the result is not a degenerate object: it is a
reading in the ordinary sense, and every step of §4 applies to it.


4. The steps
------------

There is EXACTLY ONE CONTRACTION STEP CASE (with two options) and EXACTLY ONE
REFLECTION STEP CASE. These are all the operations.

Every step acts at exactly one pointer, actPtr, and leaves every other pointer
of Pr untouched.


4.1 Contraction step
--------------------

    Reading R = (G(t), Pr)

CONTRACTION WORKS WITH TWO PLACES AND RELATES THEM. Both places are prepared
before the step: one is given by the selected entity or by the type of the
selected closed reading, the other is where the user stays (actPtr).

BOTH OPTIONS BUILD G(app(t1, t2)), with [t1] == A and [t2] == B. They differ only
in WHICH of the two actPtr designates: t1 in option 1, t2 in option 2. So A and B
name POSITIONS in the built term, not the option, and the contraction reads the
same way either way — A asks, B answers. What differs is where the user stood
when the question was put, and hence whether the type moves.

  This matters beyond §4.1: it is what lets the ontology-side type test of §8.6 be
  stated ONCE for both options.

The user selects one of two options.

  OPTION 1 —  
      actPtr points to G(t1), where [t1] == A
      the user selects some G(t2) with [t2] == B

      contract( G(t1), G(t2) )
              G(t2) is an ENTITY or a CLOSED READING.

      interpreted as:  move from A to B, or
                       A is the question and B is the answer to it.

      Effect:
          G(t3) is G(t) with G(t1) replaced by G(app(t1, t2)).
          Pr1   = Pr - {actPtr}  union  {pointer to G(app(t1, t2))}
          R1    = (G(t3), Pr1)

      Type at that pointer:  [app(t1,t2)] = [t2] = B.

  OPTION 2 —  
      actPtr points to G(t2), where [t1] == B
      the user selects some G(t1) with [t1] == A
  
      contract( G(t1), G(t2) )
              G(t1) is an ENTITY ONLY.

      interpreted as:  New contraction starts at A and move to B. The user stays before this step at B and after it, it still stays at B.
                       A is the question and B is the answer to it.

      Effect:
          G(t3) is G(t) with G(t2) replaced by G(app(t1, t2)).
          Pr1   = Pr - {actPtr}  union  {pointer to G(app(t1, t2))}
          R1    = (G(t3), Pr1)

      Type at that pointer:  [app(t1,t2)] = [t2] = B — UNCHANGED.

WHY OPTION 2 IS ENTITY-ONLY. Intended, not an omission. A closed reading may be
what the user ANSWERS WITH (option 1, argument position) but never what they ASK
FROM (option 2, function position). The two options are therefore NOT duals over a
common operand domain, and the summary table of §5 shows that.

  Costs nothing formally: application shapes remain COMPLETE (§7.1 — reflection is
  what supplies pointer access, and option 1 still takes closed readings), and the
  argument-position-sharing construction of §7.2 uses option 2 only with FRESH
  ENTITIES, so it is unaffected.

  It also removes the harder half of the ontology-side problem. In option 2 the
  operand lands in function position, exactly where the rule-1 redex is, rather
  than at a fresh position — so combining a grafted reading's ontology set there
  would have had to reconcile two sets over the SAME material. Only option 1's
  case remains, where the operand occupies a position the current ontologies say
  nothing about (§8.6).

WHY OPTION 2's TYPE IS UNCHANGED. The contraction runs A -> B either way, but in
option 2 the user was ALREADY at B and stays there: the selected A is placed in
function position, becoming context behind the position the user occupies. So the
type reporting B is not a failure to act — the act was putting the question, and
the answer is where the user already stood.

  Both options therefore read the same way — A asks, B answers — and differ only
  in where the user stood when the question was put. That is why §5's table can
  state one type rule for both.

THE CLOSED READING CONTRIBUTES ONLY ITS TYPE. A closed reading has no pointer
set (§1), so there is nothing to adopt from it and nothing to discard. This is why
option 1 is stated uniformly for entities and closed readings: the two differ only
in how large a graph is grafted, never in what happens to Pr.

The two options are the two ways one arrow can be used, distinguished by where
the user stays. Option 1 applies when the user is at the arrow's SUBJECT (A) and
moves along it; option 2 when the user is at its OBJECT (B) and reaches back to
the subject. A given arrow and pointer therefore generally admit only one of them
— both only when A == B.


4.2 Reflection step
-------------------

    Reading R = (G(t), Pr)
    actPtr points to G(t), where [t] == C
    the user selects  A -> B,  where A and B are both ENTITIES NO closed reading may be chosen for either.

    interpreted as: the user stays at C. The user moves neither to A nor to B,
    but REFLECTS A and B — reflecting A as the question and B as the answer to
    that question.

  BOTH OPERANDS ARE ENTITIES, and this is not the same restriction as option 2's
  (§4.1). There it is a choice about what may serve as an answer. Here it follows
  from the step itself: reflection GRAFTS NOTHING, so A and B are only TYPES cast
  onto the two occurrences (below). A closed reading could contribute only its
  type, which is to say nothing an entity does not already contribute — so
  admitting one would be meaningless rather than merely disallowed.

      Effect:
          the new reading is  G( app(t, t) ),  where t IS SHARED BY A NEW
          FAN-IN: it occurs twice in the term but exists once in the graph.

          Pr1 = Pr - {actPtr}  union
                { pointer to the edge at the LEFT-UP  aux port of the fan-in,
                  pointer to the edge at the RIGHT-UP aux port of the fan-in }

          R1 = (G(app(t,t)), Pr1)

The step introduces NO fresh variables. Both branches ARE the shared subject t;
nothing is placed above it.

REFLECTION CASTS THE TYPE OF THE SHARED TERM. t is shared, but the LEFT-UP edge is
of type A and the RIGHT-UP edge is of type B. So one and the same G(t) reads as A
through one occurrence and as B through the other, while its own type was C.

  THIS BREAKS THE TYPING OF §2, deliberately. What breaks is not [app(a,b)] = [b]
  but the tacit assumption that a SUBGRAPH HAS A TYPE AT ALL:

    - TYPE AT A POINTER remains well defined (invariant I3): a pointer designates
      an occurrence, and the occurrence carries the type.
    - [t] ALONE is no longer a function of the term for shared material. The
      normative `term_utils._term_type` computes from a term with no notion of
      which occurrence one arrived through, so IT IS INSUFFICIENT for reflected
      material and needs an occurrence argument.
    - Contraction's precondition [t1] == A (§4.1) must therefore be read as a
      condition on the POINTER'S OCCURRENCE, not on the subterm as such.

  BUT THE CAST DOES NOT DRIVE NAVIGATION. The legal moves
  offered at a pointer are built from the SHARED SUBTERM'S OWN TYPE C, whichever of
  the two occurrences is selected — not from that occurrence's cast type. So the
  cast is RECORDED on the occurrence but does not decide which arrows are
  available: after reflecting A -> B at a subterm of type C, both branches continue
  from C.

    Consequence to keep in view: A and B are then reflected WITHOUT being entered.
    That is exactly §4.2's reading — the user "moves neither to A nor to B" — so the
    cast records what was reflected, while C remains where the user stands and
    therefore what they can see (K1). It also means the O2 question (whether the
    cast is constrained by C) does not affect navigation either way.

  This aligns reading types with the geometry the reducer already has: in GAL an
  edge carries no intrinsic direction either — direction is RECOVERED per
  traversal from the context on the rightmost wire, and the two shared
  occurrences are distinguished by exactly the fan branch (grey vs black) that
  distinguishes their contexts. Type now behaves the same way: recovered per
  occurrence, not stored. See the sibling skill
  `understand-bus-optimal-reduction`.

  [OPEN — O2] Nothing relates A and B to C. After the step C appears nowhere in
  the term, so the cast is by fiat and reflection may retype a shared subterm to
  anything. A legality test on arrows out of C (EXISTS_RELATION(C,A) and
  EXISTS_RELATION(C,B)) is still available as a PRECONDITION, but nothing holds
  the result to it, and §7.4's knowledge-graph-realisability claim needs
  qualifying accordingly.

WHERE THE NEW POINTERS POINT, AND WHY IT MATTERS. They designate the EDGES AT THE
FAN-IN'S AUXILIARY PORTS — that is, the two OCCURRENCES OF t. A later contraction
at such a pointer therefore acts on the shared occurrence itself: option 2 grafts
AROUND it, which is what makes §7.2 work. This is the single most consequential
detail of this step.

Reflection is the only operation that SHARES, and the only one that INCREASES the
number of pointers. Because app(t,t) DUPLICATES THE WHOLE POINTED SUBTERM and
yields a pointer on each copy, it is also what supplies access to independent
positions — see §7.1, where this, and not operand grafting, is what makes the
calculus shape-complete.

Pr NEVER SHRINKS: contraction re-points actPtr, reflection replaces it with two,
and no operation removes a pointer. So Pr grows monotonically over a reading and
is never empty.

Correspondence to code: the sharing fan-in built by `_Compiler._fan_in`
(`optimal_lambda/compile.py`) is an arity-3 INTERNAL fan marked on the OFFSET
wire, with ports `principal` / `grey` / `black`. LEFT-UP and RIGHT-UP aux ports
are `grey` and `black`; `principal` carries the shared subject t. The OFFSET
placement is what makes the two uses DUPLICATE rather than annihilate when they
meet the syntactic lambda/application fans on the COMMAND wire — see the sibling
skill `understand-bus-optimal-reduction` §3.


5. Summary
----------

  BOTH CONTRACTION OPTIONS BUILD app(t1, t2), with [t1] == A and [t2] == B. They
  differ only in WHICH of the two actPtr designates — so A and B name POSITIONS
  in the built term, not the option.

               contraction opt 1   contraction opt 2   reflection
  actPtr at    t1  [A]             t2  [B]             t  [C]
  selects      t2, [t2]==B         t1, [t1]==A         A -> B
  operand      entity OR           ENTITY ONLY         (none — A and B are
                 closed reading                          cast, not built)
  builds       app(t1,t2)          app(t1,t2)          app(t, t)
                                                         with t SHARED
  type after   B                   B (unchanged)       A on the LEFT-UP edge,
                                                         B on the RIGHT-UP edge
                                                         (one shared t, CAST)
  pointers     actPtr re-pointed   actPtr re-pointed   actPtr -> two aux-port
                                                         pointers
  |Pr| changes 0                   0                   +1
  reading      A asks, B answers   A asks, B answers   C stays; A and B are
                 reader MOVES        reader STAYS        reflected as Q and A

  THE GOVERNING PRINCIPLE, from [app(a,b)] = [b]: the ARGUMENT determines the
  resulting type, so both options leave the type at B — what differs is whether
  the reader was already there. Option 1's actPtr was at A and moves to B; option
  2's was at B and stays, the operand becoming context in function position
  behind it.

  BOTH OPTIONS READ THE SAME WAY: A asks, B answers. The asymmetry is not in the
  question but in where the user stood when it was put.

  THE OPTIONS ARE NOT DUALS. Only option 1 accepts a closed reading (§4.1): a
  closed reading may be what the user answers WITH, never what they ask FROM.

  REFLECTION IS THE EXCEPTION: it does not derive the branch types from the term
  but CASTS them onto the two occurrences of one shared subject (§4.2). It is the
  only operation that shares, the only one that increases |Pr|, and the only one
  that breaks the typing of §2.

  Contraction relates TWO PLACES into one claim. Reflection forks ONE place into
  two over a shared subject. Those are all the moves.


6. Invariants
-------------

  I1  Every pointer of Pr designates an edge of the CURRENT G(t). Every step
      re-establishes this for the pointers it touches.

  I2  Every step acts at exactly one pointer and leaves all others untouched.
      Contraction re-points actPtr; reflection consumes it and adds two. No
      operation removes a pointer, so Pr grows monotonically and is never empty.

  I3  The type at a pointer is well defined — but as a property of the OCCURRENCE
      the pointer designates, not of the subterm alone: reflection casts A and B
      onto the two occurrences of one shared t (§4.2). For unreflected material
      the three rules of §2 compute it.

  I4  A contraction is legal only if the type precondition holds: [t1] == A at the
      pointer's occurrence, with the selected G(t2) of type B. Which option is
      offered follows from whether the user stays at the arrow's subject or its
      object.

  I5  Reflection never duplicates its subject: after the step exactly one t exists
      in the graph, reached through the fan-in.

  I6  A reading contains no abstractions (§7).


7. What a reading can build
---------------------------

Scope: applicative terms over variables (t ::= x | (t t)) — no abstractions —
plus sharing. Verified by exhaustive enumeration to 6 leaves, and for §7.2 by
tracing the construction on an explicit DAG.

7.1 APPLICATION SHAPES: COMPLETE.

  Every binary-tree shape is reachable (Catalan: 1, 1, 2, 5, 14, 42 — none
  missing up to 6 leaves). This holds with ENTITY OPERANDS ALONE — a closed
  reading is not required.

  WHAT MAKES IT COMPLETE IS REFLECTION. The obstruction is POINTER REACHABILITY,
  not the rewrite rules: contraction re-points actPtr at the new application and
  never ADDS a pointer, so contraction alone leaves the user on a single spine,
  unable to grow both children of an application into compounds. Reflection breaks
  that: app(t,t) DUPLICATES THE WHOLE POINTED SUBTERM and yields a pointer on each
  copy, so both children can be developed independently.

  Verified by exhaustive enumeration to 6 leaves: with entity operands only and
  entity-only initial steps, reflection alone gives 5/5 at 4 leaves, 14/14 at 5,
  42/42 at 6.

  CLOSED READINGS AS OPERANDS ARE THEREFORE NOT WHAT MAKES THE CALCULUS
  SHAPE-COMPLETE. They remain useful — they let a user bring finished work of
  their own to the place where they already stay, without any navigation step
  (§4.1) — but the expressiveness rests on reflection.

7.2 SHARING IN ARGUMENT POSITION: CONSTRUCTIBLE.

  Reflection shares t across both branches, one occurrence in function and one in
  argument position of app(t,t). Contraction option 2 then re-parents an
  occurrence, because the reflection pointers designate the OCCURRENCES themselves
  (§4.2).

  Construction of G(t) with term  app(app(a,t), app(b,t)),  t shared:

    1) R  = (G(t), Pr),  actPtr points to t,  [t] == C

    2) reflection A -> B:
           R1 = ( G( app(t, t) ), Pr1 )
       t is shared by the fan-in; the two new pointers designate the two
       occurrences of t, typed A and B respectively.

    3) contraction option 2 at each of those two pointers, grafting a and b in
       FUNCTION position around the shared occurrence:
           R2 = ( G( app( app(a,t), app(b,t) ) ), Pr2 )
       t remains shared, and now sits in ARGUMENT position of both app(a,t) and
       app(b,t).

  Verified by tracing on an explicit DAG: t has fan-out 2 throughout, and in R2
  both occurrences are in argument position.

  Reflection here is a STAGING DEVICE — it creates the sharing, and contraction
  option 2 re-parents it. Because reflection emits no scaffolding, nothing needs
  discharging afterwards: the construction is just reflect, then contract twice.
  This is the pattern optimal reduction exists to exploit — a duplicated ARGUMENT
  that should be reduced once — so its constructibility matters.

7.3 WHAT REMAINS OUT OF REACH.

    (a) fan-out > 2 — one subterm with three or more distinct uses. A further
        reflection creates a NEW fan-in at its own level rather than adding a
        third parent to the existing one. Whether that distinction is
        semantically meaningful for a reading depends on what the fan-in level
        means here, as opposed to what it means for the reducer (where levels
        keep independent sharings from interfering). See O3.

    (b) cyclic sharing — every step grafts new material into a graph built
        bottom-up, so the result is always a DAG.

    (c) late or arbitrary co-reference — recognising that two positions reached
        independently are the same thing. The §7.2 route shares only what one
        reflection staged; it cannot join two positions that were never forked
        from a common subject. Whether this is wanted is a design decision: a
        user who can share only what they staged by reflection is a coherent,
        and more constrained, notion. See O4.

7.4 WHAT THE KNOWLEDGE GRAPH CONSTRAINS.

  Shape completeness concerns TREE SHAPES. Which LABELLED terms are reachable is
  separately constrained by the graph: every step needs an arrow that exists,
  and for UNREFLECTED material, by [app(a,b)] = [b], the type at a pointer is its
  rightmost leaf. Those reachable labelled terms are the graph-realisable ones —
  by design.

  QUALIFICATION. Reflection CASTS types onto the two occurrences of a shared
  subject (§4.2), and nothing in the resulting term relates them to the type the
  user was standing at. So a reflected occurrence may carry a type no arrow
  licensed, and the claim above holds only for material no reflection has retyped.
  Whether to constrain the cast — e.g. to arrows out of C — is O2.


8. Ontology
-----------

The INTERPRETATION of this layer — an
ontology as an approximation of a hidden, subjective intention — is recorded in
the `reading-interpretation` skill and is not repeated here. Read it first: it is
what this section is a formalisation of.

Points marked [OPEN] are not settled by the definition.

8.1 What an ontology is
------------------------

An ontology is bound to a reading: EACH READING HAS A SET OF ONTOLOGIES.

An ontology of a reading is A MODEL IN WHICH THE READING IS VALID. Concretely, it
is a shared graph whose REDUCTION REPLAYS THE READING:

    each application of RULE 1  corresponds to a CONTRACTION in the reading
    each application of RULE 4  corresponds to a REFLECTION  in the reading

An ontology is a shared graph representing some term, together with a set of
pointers to the actual places (EDGES) where the user is staying in that graph:

    ont( G(t), P )

P corresponds ELEMENTWISE to the reading's own pointer set: each pointer of the
reading has a counterpart in each ontology.

The division of labour, from the interpretation: a READING is subjective and
contains no abstractions (I6); an ONTOLOGY is the EXPLICIT layer and is built
from the POSITIVE FORM of abstractions, G(lam a.t), which a reading never sees.
Reduction is the explicit act of ANSWERING the question.

8.1b The reading is a member of its own ontology set
-----------------------------------------------------

THE ACTUAL READING IS ALWAYS A MEMBER OF ITS OWN SET OF ONTOLOGIES, and it is
kept in step with the reading after every step.

It qualifies trivially: an ontology is a model in which the reading is valid, and
the reading contains exactly the material the user built, so every structural
condition on that material holds by construction.

  IT NEVER FIRES. A reading contains no abstractions (I6), so nothing in it can
  reduce: no R1, no R4. It is therefore the DEGENERATE model — it never
  contradicts what the user did and never accounts for it either. Under O8's
  ranking it sorts below any model whose hypotheses actually paid off.

  IT IS MAINTAINED BY MIRRORING, not by §8.6/§8.7. Those rules test a CANDIDATE
  model against a step; this member IS the step, so there is nothing to test.
  After each reading step it is simply refreshed from the reading's current term
  and pointer set. It must be kept OUT of the update rules — running it through
  them would ask whether the reading models itself, which it does by definition.

  CONSEQUENCE FOR THE EMPTY SET (§8.8). While a reading exists its set is never
  empty, so emptiness no longer signals "no valid model". What carries that
  signal is the absence of any CANDIDATE besides the reading itself: nothing
  available accounts for what the user is doing. The reading's own member does not
  mask that signal, because it never fires.

8.2 Vocabulary: which side of an abstraction
---------------------------------------------

The interpretation distinguishes two sides of an abstraction, and this section
needs both, so they are named apart:

    TITLE            the negative form — expresses the BREAKING (loss of sense).
                     What a READING sees and contracts, exactly like an entity.
    POSITIVE FORM    G(lam a.t) — the form/context of the breaking. Invisible to
                     a reading; it is what ONTOLOGIES are built from.

Where enrichment (§8.4) "takes all abstractions of type A", it means the POSITIVE
FORM of every abstraction whose type is A. Note that abstracting RETYPES:

    [ G(lam a.t) ] == [a]     which DIFFERS from [t]

so "of type A" is a test on the BOUND VARIABLE, not on the body. This is what
makes "all abstractions of type A" a finite, concrete lookup
(cf. `widgets._record_contains_iri`, which tests `term.var.iri == iri`).

8.3 Initialisation
-------------------

    after the initial step, form (a) — user selects a type A:

        ontologies = { ont( G(a), {p} ) },  p pointing at the edge G(a)

    after the initial step, form (b) — user re-opens a closed reading R1:

        ontologies = the ontologies of R1

    Then enrich (§8.4).

FORM (b) IS WELL DEFINED: ONTOLOGIES ARE STORED WHEN A READING IS SAVED. Closing drops only the pointer set, not the ontologies
(§1), so "the ontologies of R1" is a stored set, not something to re-derive.

  The pointers of those inherited ontologies must correspond elementwise (§8.1) to
  the re-opened reading's single ROOT pointer — the same collapse-to-root that
  closing performed on the reading's own pointer set. An implementation may either
  store each ontology's root pointer with it, or re-mint it on re-opening as §3(b)
  does for the reading.

  NOTE THE PERSISTENCE PREREQUISITE recorded in §1: the existing (name, term)
  schema does not hold ontology sets, so it must be extended before this
  initialisation or §8.6's closed-reading operand can run.

8.4 Enrichment
---------------

After EVERY reading step (initial, contraction or reflection) the ontology set is
enriched: wherever a pointed subterm is a VARIABLE sitting in an application, the
positive form of an abstraction of matching type may be substituted for it,
yielding a further candidate ontology.

    method enrichOnts(ontologies):
        for each ont in SNAPSHOT(ontologies):        # see note on the snapshot
            enrichOnt(ont)

    method enrichOnt( ont(G(t), P) ):
        p = headOf(P)
        if p == null: return
        let p point to the subgraph G(tp)

        enrichOnt( ont(G(t), P - p) )                # the remaining pointers

        # (a) tp in FUNCTION position of an application
        if G(t) has a subgraph G(app(tp, t2)) and tp is a variable:
            for each abstraction qp with [qp] == [tp]:
                let G(tnew) be G(t) with G(tp) replaced by G(qp)
                let pqp point to qp
                ontologies = ontologies union
                             { ont( G(tnew), (P - p) union {pqp} ) }
                enrichOnt( ont(G(tnew), P - p) )

        # (b) tp in ARGUMENT position, function side t1 a variable
        if t has a subtree app(t1, tp) and t1 is a variable:
            for each abstraction qt1 with [qt1] == [t1]:
                let G(tnew) be G(t) with G(t1) replaced by G(qt1)
                ontologies = ontologies union { ont( G(tnew), P ) }
                enrichOnt( ont(G(tnew), P - p) )

THE SNAPSHOT MATTERS. enrichOnts iterates the ontology set while enrichOnt adds
to it. The iteration must be over a SNAPSHOT taken before enrichment begins;
otherwise newly created ontologies are re-enriched in the same pass, and since
each substitution can create fresh material that matches again, the pass need not
terminate.

TERMINATION. Given the snapshot, the recursion is strictly on the SHRINKING
pointer set: every recursive call passes P - p, and a newly created ontology is
enriched only for the REMAINING pointers. No abstraction is ever substituted
inside an abstraction introduced during the same enrichment. Depth is bounded by
|P|.

THE ASYMMETRY BETWEEN (a) AND (b) is deliberate and follows the argument/function
principle of §5. In (a) the substituted abstraction becomes a NEW POSITION — pqp
joins the set, replacing p. In (b) the abstraction replaces material in FUNCTION
position, so the pointer set is left as P. Compare [app(a,b)] = [b].

ENRICHMENT COVERS THE ABSTRACTION TITLE OPERAND. When the
contraction operand is an abstraction TITLE (§4.1) rather than an entity, the
ontology side needs NO case of its own in §8.6: the title's counterpart in the
explicit layer is that abstraction's POSITIVE FORM (§8.2), and substituting it at
the pointer is exactly what enrichment does. So the reading contracts the title,
and enrichment supplies the positive form on the same step.

  One consequence for implementers: where the user NAMED a specific abstraction,
  enrichment must substitute THAT ONE, not branch over every abstraction of
  matching type. The loops above quantify over all abstractions of a type because
  nothing has been named; a title names one, so the quantifier collapses to it.

8.5 Pointer stability, and which reductions are silent
------------------------------------------------------

The ontology layer is the only layer that REDUCES (the reading never does), so
ontology pointers must survive rule applications.

  (1) UNDER ENRICHMENT. If p points to a and a is replaced by the positive form
      lamA with [a] == [lamA], then the new ontology's pointer set contains a
      pointer to lamA. The pointer FOLLOWS THE MATERIAL it designates — which is
      why case (a) above replaces p by pqp rather than dropping it.

  (2) UNDER REDUCTION. Only two configurations are considered: a pointer of P
      points to an abstraction, and immediately above that edge is either

        - a fan-in on the SAME (RIGHTMOST) wire -> RULE 1 is applicable, or
        - a fan-in on a DIFFERENT (more-left)
          wire, i.e. the SHARING node           -> RULE 4 is applicable.

      In both configurations the reduction is NON-SILENT by (3) below — rule 1 on
      the rightmost wire, and rule 4 against a rightmost abstraction fan-out — so
      both require the user's approval, which is exactly what §8.6 and §8.7 supply.

      After RULE 4, pointers to the LEFT and RIGHT abstraction (fan-in) nodes are
      added to P and the old pointer to the abstraction is removed. After RULE 1,
      the pointer outcome is as given in §8.6.

      A POINTER MAY ALSO SIT ON A NON-ABSTRACTION, in which case no rule fires
      there. The pointer is then advanced without any reduction: §8.6's non-firing
      branch re-points it, and §8.7's non-firing branch replaces it with two
      pointers on the top edges of the sharing fan-in that must already be above
      it. Since nothing reduces, no stability question arises in those cases.

  (3) WHICH REDUCTIONS ARE SILENT — a STRUCTURAL criterion.
      A silent reduction is performed by the machine without asking; it leaves the
      pointer set untouched. Silence is decided by WHICH WIRE THE OPERATORS ARE
      MARKED ON, not by whether a pointer happens to be nearby:

        - RULES 2, 3, 5, 6 are ALWAYS silent. These are the delimiter shuffles
          (bracket/croissant); they carry no syntactic content.

        - RULE 1 is silent IFF it applies on a NON-RIGHTMOST wire. On the
          RIGHTMOST wire the two fans are the syntactic λ and @ nodes, so the
          reduction is a real beta-step — the explicit act of answering — and it
          is NOT silent.

        - RULE 4 is silent IFF the fan-out is NOT on the rightmost wire, i.e. it
          is a SHARING node. When the fan-out IS on the rightmost wire it is an
          ABSTRACTION node, the duplication is the reflection itself, and it is
          NOT silent.

      EVERY NON-SILENT REDUCTION MUST BE APPROVED BY THE USER DURING READING.
      That is the whole coupling between the two layers: rule 1 on the rightmost
      wire is a CONTRACTION the user performed (§8.6), rule 4 with a rightmost
      fan-out is a REFLECTION the user performed (§8.7). The ontology layer may
      never fire either on its own initiative; it fires them only in step with the
      reading, which is what makes the ontology a MODEL OF THAT READING rather
      than an independent computation.

      WHY "RIGHTMOST" IS THE RIGHT TEST, verified against the code: a fan is
      syntactic exactly when it is marked on its rightmost wire. `compile.py`
      builds the λ and @ fans as `new_fan(2, 1, ...)` — width 2, main 1, so
      main == width-1 — while `_fan_in` builds the sharing fan as
      `new_fan(INITIAL_ROOT_WIDTH, OFFSET, ...)` — width 3, main 1, whose
      rightmost slot is 2 (COMMAND). So the single test `main == width - 1`
      separates "syntactic λ/@" from "sharing", and the two clauses above are one
      criterion stated from the two sides. This is also why read-back follows the
      COMMAND / rightmost wire and branches only at syntactic fans.


  WHICH NODES "LEFT" AND "RIGHT" ARE, after rule 4. Verified against
  `rules.py:rule4_fan_fan_diff` by tracing an actual firing: the rule REMOVES
  both fans that met principal-to-principal and creates FOUR new ones. Two of
  them are copies of the ABSTRACTION's fan, replicated onto the sharing fan's
  grey and black branches; their principals reach what were the sharing fan's
  grey/black externals, and they carry the abstraction's `role` and `iri`. THOSE
  TWO are the left and right nodes the new pointers designate. The other two are
  copies of the SHARING fan and are not pointer targets.

  NO CASCADE RISK. A pointer elsewhere in P cannot be disturbed by a step acting
  at another pointer: rule 4 removes only the two fans that met, and re-attaches
  all four captured branch externals (`_capture_ports` / `_finalize`) rather than
  deleting them. Confirmed by trace: no new redex arises at the untouched
  anchors. So every reading step disturbs exactly the one pointer it acts at,
  preserving the elementwise correspondence of §8.1.

  A SECOND REASON THERE IS NO CASCADE, from (3): a silent cascade can never
  perform a beta-step or a reflection, because those are exactly the NON-SILENT
  cases. So "then all possible silent reductions" in §8.6 and §8.7 cannot advance
  the ontology past the reading — it can only shuffle delimiters and resolve
  sharing that carries no syntactic content. The user's approval gates every
  reduction that means anything.

8.6 Update on a contraction step  (A -> B at actPtr)
-----------------------------------------------------

  TWO BRANCHES PER ONTOLOGY: one where RULE 1 fires and one where it does not.
  The non-firing ontology is KEPT — that is SILENCE, not contradiction.

  OPTION 1 — the reader's position moves to B.
              The user selects an entity B, or a closed reading R(G(tb)) with
              [R(G(tb))] == B.

    newOntologies = {}
    for each ont(G(t), P) in ontologies:
        take p from P corresponding to actPtr in the reading

        if t contains a subtree app(ta, tb) with p pointing to ta,
           [ta] == A and [tb] == B:

            if RULE 1 is NOT applicable to G(t) at p:        # ta not abstraction
                newOntologies = newOntologies union
                    { ont( G(t), (P - {p}) union {pointer to app(ta, tb)} ) }

            if RULE 1 IS applicable to G(t) at p:            # ta is abstraction

                if the user selected an ENTITY B:
                    let pb point to tb
                    apply rule 1, then all possible silent reductions (§8.5(3))
                        -> G(t1)
                    newOntologies = newOntologies union
                                    { ont( G(t1), (P - {p}) union {pb} ) }

                if the user selected a CLOSED READING R(G(tb1)),
                   [R(G(tb1))] == B:

                    if tb is a variable b:
                        for each ont(G(tb2)) in the ontology set of R(G(tb1)):
                            replace the variable b by G(tb2), then apply rule 1
                            to the redex app(G(ta), G(tb2)), then all possible
                            silent reductions (§8.5(3))    -> G(t1)
                            newOntologies = newOntologies union
                                { ont( G(t1), (P - {p}) union
                                              {pointer to tb2} ) }

                    if tb is NOT a variable:
                        if tb is one of R(G(tb1))'s own ontology-terms:
                            apply rule 1 to the redex app(G(ta), G(tb)), then all
                            possible silent reductions (§8.5(3))    -> G(t1)
                            newOntologies = newOntologies union
                                { ont( G(t1), (P - {p}) union
                                              {pointer to tb} ) }

    ontologies = newOntologies
    then enrich (§8.4)

  OPTION 2 —

    newOntologies = {}
    for each ont(G(t), P) in ontologies:
        take p from P corresponding to actPtr in the reading

        if t contains a subtree app(ta, tb) with p pointing to tb,
           [ta] == A and [tb] == B:

            if RULE 1 is NOT applicable at app(ta, tb):      # ta not abstraction                
                newOntologies = newOntologies union { ont( G(t), (P - {p}) union {pointer to G(app(ta, tb))} ) }

            if RULE 1 IS applicable at the REDEX, which is at ta:
                apply rule 1, then all possible silent reductions (§8.5(3))
                    -> G(t1)
                newOntologies = newOntologies union { ont( G(t1), P ) }

    ontologies = newOntologies
    then enrich (§8.4)


  NOTE ON OPTION 2, TWO POINTS. First, the pointer p designates tb, the ARGUMENT,
  whereas the abstraction — and hence the RULE 1 REDEX — is at ta. So the
  applicability test is at ta, NOT at p; only the surviving pointer is derived
  from p. (Option 1 is the case where the two coincide.) Second, P is carried
  over UNCHANGED in BOTH branches because the reader does not move (§4.1) — but
  the pointers must be REBASED onto G(t1) where a reduction happened: "unchanged"
  means the same positions, not the same edge identities.

  REVIEW NOTES ON THIS DEFINITION. Numbered RN* to avoid collision with the
  reducer's rules R1-R6. Each needs a ruling.

  (RN1) WHAT NON-FIRING SURVIVAL COSTS — a narrow leak, not a general one.

       THE OUTER STRUCTURAL TEST IS THE STRONG GATE. It requires the ontology TO ALREADY CONTAIN THE MATERIAL THE
       READING IS ABOUT TO BUILD: an app(ta, tb) at the pointer with [tb] equal to
       the contraction's target type. Enrichment (§8.4) substitutes abstractions
       for variables but NEVER ADDS APPLICATIONS, so an ontology satisfies this
       only if some abstraction BODY supplies the needed argument position.
       Passing it at every step is therefore demanding, not a formality.

       WORKED COUNTEREXAMPLE. Reading (a b); the user contracts B -> C, giving
       ((a b) c). Available abstractions: (lam a).(a d), (lam b).(b e),
       (lam c).(c f). The candidate ((lam b).(b e)) d is an ontology for (a b),
       but at the B -> C step the pointer designates the WHOLE term, which has no
       enclosing application — so the outer test fails and the ontology IS
       REFUTED. None of the three bodies supplies a c in argument position, so
       every candidate is refuted here and the set goes EMPTY (§8.8) — the reading
       has outrun the available abstractions.

       WHAT THE LEAK ACTUALLY IS. The non-firing branch matters only for
       ontologies that ALREADY PASS the outer test. Those have the right shape at
       the pointer; previously they additionally needed ta to be an abstraction.
       Now they do not, so the rule admits ontologies with the right SHAPE but no
       HYPOTHESIS at that position. That category is real but narrow, and keeping
       it is the right call: proposing nothing at a position is SILENCE, not
       contradiction.

       STILL WORTH DECIDING (O8): whether to RECORD which branch was taken, e.g.
       a count of firings per ontology, so that "survivors are better
       approximations" (the interpretation's claim) tracks CONFIRMATION rather
       than mere survival. That is a ranking refinement, not a fix for a hole.

  (RN2) OPTION 1's CLOSED-READING CASE HAS NO FALLTHROUGH. If tb is not a variable
       AND not one of R1's ontology-terms, neither inner branch fires and the
       ontology is dropped — contradicting the non-firing branch just added.
       Either add an else that keeps it, or state that this case refutes.

  (RN3) "tb IS ONE OF R1's ONTOLOGY-TERMS" NEEDS RESTATING. tb is a subterm of the
       CURRENT ontology's graph, so it cannot literally be an ontology of R1 —
       those are different graphs. The intended test is presumably that tb ALREADY
       IS one of R1's ontology-terms, having arrived by an earlier graft or
       enrichment. As written it compares incomparable things.

  (RN4) THE CLOSED-READING CASE IS A CARTESIAN PRODUCT. It pairs each current
       ontology with each of R1's, so |newOntologies| may reach k*m at one step.
       That is a legitimate decision, but it is the one place the set can grow
       multiplicatively, and it is what O7 has to weigh against the outer test's
       heavy refutation.

  (RN5) POINTER AFTER REDUCTION. Both closed-reading branches end with a "pointer
       to tb2" / "pointer to tb", but rule 1 plus silent reductions may have
       rewritten that material. The pointer must designate the RESULT at that
       position, per the rebasing note above.

  OPTION 2 HAS NO CLOSED-READING CASE BECAUSE THE STEP ADMITS NO SUCH OPERAND. Option 2 is ENTITY-ONLY by intent — see §4.1, where the
  restriction now belongs and is justified. So the absence above is correct and
  complete, not a gap. It also means the only ontology-side combining problem is
  option 1's, where the operand occupies a position the current ontologies say
  nothing about; option 2 would have required reconciling two sets over the SAME
  material, since its operand lands in function position exactly where the rule-1
  redex is.

  THE ABSTRACTION TITLE OPERAND NEEDS NO CASE HERE EITHER.
  A title's counterpart in the explicit layer is that abstraction's positive form,
  and substituting it at the pointer is what ENRICHMENT already does — see the note
  at the end of §8.4, including the one caveat: a named title collapses
  enrichment's quantifier to that single abstraction.

  EVERY OPERAND OF §4.1 IS COVERED on the ontology side: entity and closed reading
  by §8.6 above, abstraction title by enrichment. What remains for this section is
  review notes RN2, RN3 and RN5 — corrections to the pseudocode as written, not
  open design questions.

8.7 Update on a reflection step  (A -> B at actPtr, [t_at_actPtr] == C)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

  The user stays at C and reflects A against B; toc is the subterm the ontology
  pointer designates.

  Like §8.6 it has TWO BRANCHES — one where the rule fires and one where it does
  not — but here the non-firing branch carries its own structural requirement: the
  sharing fan-in must ALREADY be in place above toc.

    newOntologies = {}
    for each ont(G(t), P) in ontologies:
        take p from P corresponding to actPtr in the reading;
            let p point to toc

        if toc is an abstraction
           and RULE 4 is applicable at p
           and t contains a subtree app(ta, tb) with [ta] == A, [tb] == B,
               [toc] == C
           and G(toc) IS ALREADY SHARED as a subterm in both ta and tb:
            apply rule 4, then all possible silent reductions (§8.5(3))
                -> G(t1)
            newOntologies = newOntologies union
                { ont( G(t1), (P - {p}) union
                              { pointer to the LEFT  abstraction fan-in node,
                                pointer to the RIGHT abstraction fan-in node } ) }

        if toc is NOT an abstraction
           and above toc is a SHARING FAN-IN (on the different wire, on the left)
           and t contains a subtree app(ta, tb) with [ta] == A, [tb] == B,
               [toc] == C
           and G(toc) IS ALREADY SHARED as a subterm in both ta and tb:
            newOntologies = newOntologies union
                { ont( G(t), (P - {p}) union
                              { pointer to the TOP LEFT  fan-in edge,
                                pointer to the TOP RIGHT fan-in edge } ) }

    ontologies = newOntologies
    then enrich (§8.4)

  NOTE THE ASYMMETRY BETWEEN THE BRANCHES, which is the point of the second one.
  When toc is an abstraction the rule FIRES: rule 4 rewrites the graph, so the new
  pointers designate NODES among the four fans it creates (§8.5). When toc is not
  an abstraction NOTHING REDUCES: G(t) is unchanged and the new pointers designate
  the EDGES at the existing fan-in's two top ports. So the second branch advances
  the pointer set to mirror the reading's fork without performing any reduction —
  the ontology has the required SHARING but proposes no hypothesis at that
  position. This is the reflection counterpart of §8.6's non-firing branch
  (contradicted vs merely silent), and unlike §8.6 it still demands real structure:
  an ontology with no sharing fan-in above toc fails BOTH branches and is refuted.

  TWO SEPARATE TESTS, NOT ONE. "Rule 4 is applicable" and "G(toc) is already
  shared in both" are INDEPENDENT conditions, and the second is STRICTER than the
  reducer's own. Verified against `rules.py:rule4_fan_fan_diff`: it fires whenever
  two fans meet principal-to-principal on DIFFERENT main wires, and its EFFECT is
  to replicate each onto the other's grey/black branches — the reducer CREATES
  duplication rather than requiring it. The already-shared requirement is
  therefore an EXTRA FILTER layered on redex detection and must be implemented
  separately, not read off `find_redexes`. It is a precondition BY DECISION, and
  it matches the interpretation: reflection reflects from WITHIN the contraction,
  so the sharing pre-exists.

  THE "DIFFERENT WIRE, ON THE LEFT" CONDITION in the second branch is the same
  configuration §8.5(2) identifies as the rule-4 case: a fan-in on a more-left
  wire than the syntactic fans, i.e. the OFFSET-marked sharing fan of
  `_Compiler._fan_in`. It is what makes the two uses DUPLICATE rather than
  annihilate.

  Which nodes the two new pointers designate in the FIRING branch: see §8.5 — the
  two copies of the abstraction's fan, identified by their `role` and `iri`.

8.8 Refutation, and the empty set
----------------------------------

Every update rule REPLACES the ontology set with newOntologies, so AN ONTOLOGY IS
DISCARDED BY NEVER BEING ADDED. There is no discard statement; refutation is a
side effect of the update rules' conditions, and its strength is exactly their
strictness.

WHAT REFUTES:

  - failing the OUTER STRUCTURAL TEST — no subtree app(ta,tb) at the pointer with
    the required types. THIS IS THE STRONG GATE. It demands that the ontology
    ALREADY CONTAIN the material the reading is about to build, and since
    enrichment substitutes abstractions but never adds applications (§8.4), an
    ontology passes only if some abstraction BODY supplies the needed argument
    position. Most candidates fail it as a reading proceeds — see the worked
    counterexample in review note RN1 (§8.6), where every available abstraction is
    refuted at a single step and the set goes empty.
  - in §8.7 (reflection), failing BOTH branches: the ontology has neither an
    abstraction at the pointer with rule 4 applicable, NOR an existing sharing
    fan-in above toc. Reflection therefore refutes harder than contraction, since
    both of its branches demand real structure — the required sharing must already
    be present either way.

WHAT DOES NOT REFUTE: an ontology which PASSES the outer test but in which RULE 1
does not fire. §8.6 KEEPS it. The distinction is between an ontology
CONTRADICTED by what the user did and one merely SILENT about it — proposing
nothing at a position is not the same as being falsified there. This admits
ontologies with the right SHAPE but no HYPOTHESIS at the pointer: a real but narrow
category (RN1).

STILL TO DECIDE (O8): whether to record which branch was taken, so that the
interpretation's "survivors are better approximations" tracks CONFIRMATION rather
than mere survival. A ranking refinement, not a fix for a hole.

THE SIGNAL IS THE ABSENCE OF CANDIDATES, NOT AN EMPTY SET. Since the reading is
itself a member (§8.1b), the set is never empty while a reading exists. What is
MEANINGFUL is a set holding only the reading's own mirror: NO AVAILABLE EXPLICIT
MATERIAL ACCOUNTS FOR WHAT THIS USER IS DOING — the reading has outrun the
abstractions available to it. It is a distinguished state to be SURFACED to the
user, and deliberately NOT an assertion failure. The reading's own member cannot
mask it, since that member never fires.


9. Worked examples
------------------

9.1 A short reading.

  Let the graph contain  Gene -encodes-> Protein,  Protein -binds-> Ligand,
  Ligand -hasAffinity-> Affinity,  Ligand -hasStructure-> Structure.

    initial step: the user selects Protein.
        t  = p0                              [t] = Protein
        Pr = {ptr(p0)}

    contraction, option 2, with the entity Gene along Gene -> Protein:
        actPtr is at Protein — the arrow's OBJECT — so the user reaches back to
        its subject. Gene (t1) goes into function position; the user stays at
        Protein (t2) and the type does not move.
        t  = app(g0, p0)                     [t] = Protein — unchanged
        Pr = {ptr(app(g0,p0))}
        Gene asks; Protein answers.

    contraction, option 1, with the entity Ligand along Protein -> Ligand:
        t  = app( app(g0,p0), l0 )           [t] = Ligand
        Pr = {ptr(that app)}
        Protein asks; Ligand answers.

    reflection, Affinity -> Structure:
        t  = app( l, l ),  l = app(app(g0,p0), l0), SHARED by the new fan-in
        Pr = {ptr to left-up aux port, ptr to right-up aux port}
        The user stays at Ligand and reflects Affinity as question against
        Structure as answer. The two occurrences of l are CAST to Affinity
        (left-up) and Structure (right-up), though l's own type was Ligand.

    contraction, option 1, at the left-up pointer along Affinity -> Measurement:
        that occurrence becomes app(l, meas); the right-up pointer is untouched.
        Legality is read at the OCCURRENCE, which carries Affinity — not at l,
        whose own type is Ligand.

9.2 Argument-position sharing.

  See §7.2, which is a worked example in its own right.


10. Open points
---------------

Numbered O1..O9. Resolved questions are not listed here — where a decision was
made it is stated in the section it governs.

  O1  HOW THE TYPING RULES ACCOUNT FOR THE REFLECTION CAST (§4.2). Since a shared
      subterm may read as A through one occurrence and B through another, [t] is
      not a function of the term, and `term_utils._term_type` needs an OCCURRENCE
      argument. §2's three rules cover unreflected material only. The restatement
      is mechanical but has not been made.

  O2  WHETHER THE REFLECTION CAST IS CONSTRAINED (§4.2, §7.4). Nothing relates A
      and B to the type C the user was standing at, so reflection may retype a
      shared subterm to anything. Candidate constraint: require arrows out of C,
      i.e. EXISTS_RELATION(C,A) and EXISTS_RELATION(C,B). Bears directly on the
      knowledge-graph-realisability claim of §7.4.

  O3  THE FAN-IN LEVEL'S MEANING for a reading, bearing on §7.3(a): a further
      reflection creates a NEW fan-in at its own level rather than adding a third
      parent, so fan-out > 2 is not reachable. Whether that distinction is
      semantically meaningful for a reading — as opposed to for the reducer, where
      levels keep independent sharings from interfering — is open.

  O4  WHETHER A MERGE / CO-REFERENCE STEP IS WANTED, to reach §7.3(c). It would
      consume two pointers and yield one — "these two things I have attended to
      separately are the same thing". It is NOT needed for argument-position
      sharing, which §7.2 constructs from the existing steps.

  O5  WHETHER THE SELECTED G(t1) MUST BE JOINED BY AN ARROW THAT EXISTS in the
      knowledge graph when it is a closed reading rather than an entity. Concerns
      OPTION 1 ONLY: option 2 is entity-only (§4.1), and an entity operand always
      has an ordinary arrow to check. A closed reading is not an entity, so the
      existence check has no evident counterpart; only the type precondition is
      checked.

  O6  COPY VS. SHARE when a closed reading is grafted (option 1 only). Copying
      keeps closed readings immutable; sharing makes later changes visible in every
      reading that grafted them. Copy is the safer default.

  O7  WHETHER THE ONTOLOGY SET NEEDS A SIZE BOUND. Enrichment terminates (§8.4)
      but branches over every abstraction of matching type at every pointer on
      every step, and option 1's closed-reading case multiplies by the operand's
      own set (§8.6 review note RN4). Against that, the OUTER STRUCTURAL TEST
      refutes heavily (§8.8) — a set can go empty in a few steps, as the
      counterexample in RN1 shows. Whether the two rates balance in practice is
      not settled, so a cap plus deterministic eviction may still be wanted; O8
      proposes what to rank by.

  O8  WHETHER TO RECORD CONFIRMATION. §8.6 keeps an ontology in which rule 1 does
      not fire — right, in distinguishing CONTRADICTED from SILENT. But then mere
      survival no longer distinguishes an ontology whose hypotheses kept paying off
      from one that stayed quiet, which is what the interpretation's "survivors are
      better approximations" asserts. Candidate: count firings per ontology, rank by
      it, and let any O7 cap evict by it. A refinement, not a fix for a hole — see
      review note RN1 in §8.6.

  O9  THE INITIAL ONTOLOGY CANNOT SURVIVE THE FIRST CONTRACTION — a tension in the
      definition, not a coding matter.

      Init (§8.3 form (a)) seeds ontologies = { ont(G(a), {p}) } — a BARE
      VARIABLE. The first contraction then applies §8.6's outer structural test,
      which demands the ontology ALREADY CONTAIN app(ta,tb) at the pointer. A
      bare variable does not, so that ontology is refuted and, unless enrichment
      has meanwhile produced a candidate that does, the set goes EMPTY on step
      one of every reading.

      But enrichment (§8.4) cannot help at init either: both its cases require
      the pointed variable to sit IN AN APPLICATION, and at init there is no
      application yet. So the seed ontology is refuted before anything can be
      substituted into it.

      AT ROOT IS AN AMBIGUITY IN §8.6's OUTER TEST: WHEN is it read?

        (i)  against the ontology BEFORE the step. The test then demands that the
             ontology ALREADY CONTAIN the application the reading is about to
             build, and it is a genuine structural filter: most candidates die on
             shape. The seed, a bare variable, fails it — hence this open point.
             THIS IS WHAT §8.8 STATES AND WHAT THE IMPLEMENTATION DOES.

        (ii) against the ontology AFTER the step, the contraction extending the
             ontology's term as well as the reading's. The seed then becomes
             app(a,b), the test passes, rule 1 does not apply (no abstraction),
             and it lands in the NON-FIRING branch — surviving as silent with its
             pointer advanced. Under (ii) a reflection-free reading is trivially
             an ontology of itself, and this open point dissolves.

      Evidence pulls both ways. For (ii): the non-firing branch re-points to
      app(ta, tb), which only makes sense if the ontology acquired that
      application — if its term were untouched there would be no new node to
      point at. For (i): the worked counterexample of RN1 refutes
      ((lam b).(b e)) d at a B -> C step because "the pointer designates the WHOLE
      term, which has no enclosing application" — a test applied BEFORE any
      extension; under (ii) that candidate would have been extended and passed.

      The choice decides what an ontology IS. Under (ii) every ontology mirrors
      the reading's shape and differs only in where abstractions were substituted,
      so refutation only ever means "the abstraction I hypothesised here did not
      reduce as needed". Under (i) shape itself is falsifiable.

      NOTE ALSO, IF (ii) IS EVER CHOSEN: §8.7 has no counterpart. Reflection
      requires the ontology to ALREADY have an abstraction at the pointer, or a
      sharing fan already above it, and refutes otherwise. Choosing (ii) for
      contraction leaves reflection still emptying the set, so the same question
      would have to be answered for §8.7 — and answering it "yes" would remove
      the property that reflection refutes harder than contraction.

      Other candidates, if the ambiguity is settled as (i): seed the set from the
      abstractions of type A directly rather than from the bare variable — note
      the seed must be an abstraction APPLIED to the first operand, since a bare
      LamAbs has no app(ta,tb) at the pointer either; or exempt the first
      contraction from the outer test; or accept that a reading has no model until
      it has built something, which is defensible under the interpretation but
      makes the empty-set signal fire on every reading's first step.
